<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="a27f95a5">&lt;p&gt;
    Determine the capabilities required for a specific project management office along&amp;nbsp;the PMO Competency Continuum
    (Gerard M. Hill) as illustrated below:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &amp;nbsp;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/tasks/resources/pmo_comp_continuum.png&quot; width=&quot;800&quot; height=&quot;423&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Establish the infrastructure to support and govern the project environment.&amp;nbsp; This may require procuring space for
    the project team and project management tools to facilitate providing the project with standard and repeatable
    processes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Smaller projects may not require specialization of roles, however as the size and complexity of the project increases,
    in order to deliver project management activities consistently and with high quality on time and on budget,
    specialization of roles may be called for.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Determine if specialists&amp;nbsp;are needed to control risks and issues, project quality, project level changes, staffing,
    maintain the schedule and its dependencies&amp;nbsp;and report status to project governance. On larger complex projects,
    workstreams within the same project may require&amp;nbsp;dedicated project management oversight beyond the capacity of a
    single project manager, requiring multiple individuals to perform project management tasks. &amp;nbsp; If so, designate
    these individuals to perform these activities.&amp;nbsp; If multiple project managers are needed for the project,
    consult&amp;nbsp;with&amp;nbsp;project leaders&amp;nbsp;to determine if the Program Management method should be leveraged by your
    project team.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
